WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.530
Imagine you're about to
be a proud new father,

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:08.530
but first, you have to
give birth to 2,000 babies.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.186
Sounds painful, right?

00:00:10.186 --> 00:00:13.640
Well, if you're a male seahorse,
that is part of the job.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.110
Turns out, there's a lot
of animal dads out there

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.750
who do all of the hard work.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.320
The hardhead catfish
takes parental sacrifice

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.707
to the extreme.

00:00:24.707 --> 00:00:28.010
To protect the eggs, he'll
hold them in his mouth.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.100
And it's not just one or two eggs.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.690
It could be as many as 65.

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:35.330
The eggs take up so much
space that he can't eat

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.707
because he might swallow one.

00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:41.598
So he'll fast for over two
months while the eggs develop.

00:00:41.598 --> 00:00:42.700
Believe it or not,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.420
the male giant water bug
takes on an even heavier load.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.500
Females lay up to 100
eggs on the male's back.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.690
He'll carry the eggs for
the next two to three weeks

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.302
as they develop.

00:00:53.302 --> 00:00:57.250
But during that time, the eggs
and mucus glue his wings down

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.370
so that he can't even fly,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.170
making him vulnerable to predators.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.700
Plus, the eggs weigh him
down so much he has to rely

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.530
on slower, easier prey like snails.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.090
Or he just doesn't eat at all.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.143
But it's worth it to ensure
his future offspring are safe.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.400
Luckily, for the water bug,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.110
he can return to his regular
life after the eggs hatch

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.060
because the babies fend for themselves.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.920
That's not the case for the male rhea.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.950
He doesn't just stick around
to raise his chicks on his own,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.870
he raises multiple families at once.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.930
During breeding season, the
male rhea will build a nest

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.331
to attract as many females as possible.

00:01:34.331 --> 00:01:37.120
He'll form a group of
about five to seven females

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.112
and mate with them that season.

00:01:39.112 --> 00:01:42.554
The females lay their eggs in
his nest and then skedaddle.

00:01:42.554 --> 00:01:43.850
Like, for good,

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.400
leaving him to raise and
defend all 30 or so chicks

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.150
until they're about six months old.

00:01:50.192 --> 00:01:53.250
Talk about the challenges
of being an only parent.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.520
Last but certainly not least,
is the male emperor penguin

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.723
who does a lot of the hard work early on.

00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:02.829
Female penguins entrust
their eggs to the males.

00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:05.790
But since penguins don't have
nests, the males have to play

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.093
a delicate balancing act.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.550
They keep the egg warm atop their feet

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.600
and away from the frozen
ground they stand on.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.760
But they can't hunt or forage for food.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.653
They have to stay put
and protect that egg.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.200
But the hard work doesn't even end there.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.540
By the time the chicks hatch,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.530
the fathers are starved from
fasting for weeks on end

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.340
but the chicks need to be fed.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.930
So, Dad manages to regurgitate some food

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.853
from his esophagus to feed
the chick until Mom returns.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.380
Delicious, thanks Dad.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.830
Love it when you cook dinner.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.384
Looks like there's a lot of
animal dads who deserve an award

00:02:45.384 --> 00:02:46.534
for Father of the Year.

